e,radius:s=120,pull:l=.35,className:d=""}){let c=(0,a.useRef)(null),u=(0,o.useReducedMotion)(),h=(0,n.useMotionValue)(0),p=(0,n.useMotionValue)(0),f=(0,i.useSpring)(h,{stiffness:180,damping:20,mass:.6}),g=(0,i.useSpring)(p,{stiffness:180,damping:20,mass:.6});return(0,t.jsx)(r.motion.div,{ref:c,onPointerMove:e=>{if(u||!c.current)return;let t=c.current.getBoundingClientRect(),a=t.left+t.width/2,r=t.top+t.height/2,n=e.clientX-a,i=e.clientY-r,o=Math.hypot(n,i);if(o>s){h.set(0),p.set(0);return}let d=1-o/s;h.set(n*l*d),p.set(i*l*d)},onPointerLeave:()=>{h.set(0),p.set(0)},style:{x:f,y:g},className:`inline-block p-6 -m-6 ${d}`,children:e})}e.s(["default",()=>s])},82356,e=>{"use strict";e.s(["CASE_STUDIES",0,[{id:"databricks-multi-source-delivery",slug:"databricks-multi-source-delivery",kind:"reference",client:"Reference architecture",category:"Data Engineering",title:"A multi-source lakehouse, released through code",short:"Databricks, release-ready",lines:["A multi-source","lakehouse.","Released through code."],desc:"A proposed Azure Databricks architecture for SQL snapshots, REST extracts and partner files: source contracts, Bronze–Silver–Gold processing, independent quality gates and reviewed releases through GitHub Actions.",galleryScope:"SQL, API and file sources. Dev, stage and prod.",stat:"One versioned release path",statLabel:"Source contracts · governed releases",techStack:["Azure Databricks","ADLS Gen2","Auto Loader","Delta Lake","Lakeflow Jobs","Lakeflow pipelines","Unity Catalog","Declarative Automation Bundles (DAB)","GitHub Actions","OIDC","Databricks SQL","Power BI"],lede:"This reference architecture brings ERP orders in Azure SQL, CRM records from a REST API and partner shipment files into one governed Azure Databricks design. Source contracts, Bronze–Silver–Gold processing and independent quality gates connect the data. Bundles and GitHub Actions define how reviewed code would reach development, staging and production.",situation:{current:{label:"Reference scenario",title:"Three sources. Different contracts.",desc:"An ERP database, a paginated CRM API and partner file deliveries expose different update, deletion and completion rules. A shared reporting model would need to reconcile those contracts and make each refresh and code change traceable."},target:{label:"Proposed design",title:"One governed path to reporting.",desc:"Complete source batches would feed Bronze and Silver, with an independent quality gate before Gold. Reviewed code and resource definitions would move through isolated environments, while deployment, runtime and storage access would use deliberately assigned identities."}},architecture:"databricks-multi-source-delivery",partsTitle:"From source contract to production",parts:[{title:"Agree how each source is complete",desc:"The proposal would extract complete Azure SQL snapshots through Spark JDBC into Parquet. A custom Python task would handle the CRM API’s pagination, rate limits, retries and documented cursor rules, landing JSON. Partner CSV deliveries would arrive in a separate ADLS path. Each source contract would define keys, schema, deletes and batch completion. Manifests and reconciliation counts would distinguish a complete extract from a partial delivery; source consistency requirements would determine the SQL extraction window.",meta:["SQL snapshots","Custom REST extraction","ADLS landing contracts"]},{title:"Retain a recoverable Bronze record",desc:"Lakeflow Jobs would schedule Auto Loader with AvailableNow to ingest completed file deliveries into Bronze Delta tables. Each invocation would process files available before it starts, using persistent checkpoints and schema locations specific to each source and environment. Immutable landing files and source, file, batch and ingestion metadata would support investigation and replay. The API extractor would advance its cursor only after durable landing. Auto Loader’s checkpointed Delta ingestion guarantees would apply to files; business duplicates would require their own rules.",meta:["Auto Loader AvailableNow","Persistent checkpoints","Bronze Delta tables"]},{title:"Validate Silver before Gold can run",desc:"A triggered Lakeflow pipeline would standardize types, timestamps and business keys, select completed snapshots and reconcile source identifiers. Expectations would enforce row rules, with an explicit quarantine branch retaining invalid records. A separate job task would check source completion, freshness, uniqueness, relationships and agreed reconciliation tolerances before allowing the Gold pipeline to run. This separation matters: a failing expectation can roll back its table update while other parallel flows succeed. The quality gate would control downstream execution across those flows.",meta:["Expectations + quarantine","Cross-source checks","Independent quality gate"]},{title:"Give reporting an explicit grain",desc:"The proposed Gold model would define order and invoice facts alongside conformed customer, product and date dimensions, with shipment facts at their own grain. Revenue, backlog and delivery measures would have documented definitions and reconciliation checks. Databricks SQL would serve the approved tables to a Power BI semantic model after the quality gate succeeds. Access and report refresh would be configured for that serving path. A batch identifier and freshness record would help readers understand which completed source deliveries each result represents.",meta:["Facts + dimensions","Databricks SQL","Power BI semantic model"]},{title:"Declare the release with its code",desc:"Declarative Automation Bundles, formerly Databricks Asset Bundles, would version the Python and SQL code, job and pipeline definitions, permissions and target settings together. Dev, stage and prod would resolve to separate workspaces, catalogs, landing paths and checkpoints. Stage and prod would use stable deployment roots; stage schedules would remain explicitly paused. The same reviewed commit and versioned artifact would be promoted. Bundles track deployed resources by workspace IDs and state, so changing bundle roots or adopting existing resources would require explicit state management.",meta:["Versioned resources","Dev / stage / prod","Stable deployment state"]},{title:"Make execution part of the release gate",desc:"GitHub Actions would run linting, unit tests and source-contract fixtures on pull requests. Trusted integration stages would validate the bundle configuration, deploy to the selected test environment, then explicitly run jobs against representative fixtures. Tests would cover partial deliveries, schema changes, duplicate records and replay, followed by Silver-to-Gold reconciliation. Bundle validation checks configuration; deployment creates or updates resources; neither proves data correctness. Production promotion would require successful stage results and its configured approval, followed by an explicit smoke check.",meta:["GitHub Actions","Validate → deploy → run","Stage tests + approval"]},{title:"Separate deployment from execution",desc:"GitHub would authenticate through workload identity federation into a deployment service principal for each environment. The production policy would match the repository’s actual OIDC subject and audience, with branch restrictions and approval on the protected GitHub environment. Target run_as settings would assign jobs and pipelines a separate runtime principal, and the deployer would receive permission to use it. GitHub token claims and approval availability would be verified for the repository and plan. This design would remove long-lived Databricks deployment secrets.",meta:["GitHub OIDC","Protected environments","Deployment + run_as identities"]},{title:"Bind access to the environment",desc:"Unity Catalog would govern the environment catalogs, tables and landing volumes. Catalog and external-location bindings would restrict production access to its intended workspace; workspace separation alone would not establish that boundary. An Azure Databricks Access Connector with a managed identity would provide ADLS access through a storage credential. Runtime principals would receive the required data, compute and secret permissions. API and database credentials would be referenced through governed connections or permissioned secret scopes, with values excluded from code and routine logs.",meta:["Unity Catalog bindings","ADLS managed identity","Runtime secret access"]},{title:"Design the recovery path alongside the run",desc:"The proposed job would record source batches, extraction cursors, task outcomes, quality results and reporting freshness, with alerts on failures and late deliveries. Checkpoints would be protected from storage cleanup, and retention would preserve the agreed replay window. Retries would reuse batch identities and be tested for duplicate effects. An operations guide would distinguish restarting a failed task, replaying retained data and redeploying an earlier code version. Data or schema recovery would require its own reviewed procedure; code rollback would not restore either automatically.",meta:["Batch evidence","Alerts + replay","Recovery procedures"]}],changes:[{tag:"Contracts",title:"Source completeness would be explicit.",desc:"Keys, delivery manifests and batch records would make incomplete extracts visible before they feed reporting."},{tag:"Quality",title:"Gold would follow a successful gate.",desc:"Recorded row checks and cross-source reconciliation would determine whether the reporting refresh can proceed."},{tag:"Releases",title:"Each promotion would carry evidence.",desc:"Reviewed code, versioned resources and executed stage tests would accompany the production release."},{tag:"Operations",title:"Access and recovery would have owners.",desc:"Assigned identities, environment boundaries and replay procedures would make the operating responsibilities concrete."}],references:[{title:"Auto Loader production behavior",href:"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/production"},{title:"Pipeline expectations and failure boundaries",href:"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/ldp/expectations"},{title:"Bundle validation, deployment and execution",href:"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/dev-tools/cli/bundle-commands"},{title:"Bundle runtime identity",href:"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/dev-tools/bundles/run-as"},{title:"GitHub federation for Azure Databricks",href:"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/dev-tools/auth/provider-github"},{title:"GitHub OIDC claims and trust conditions",href:"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/oidc"}],verifiedAt:"2026-09-06"},{id:"healthcare-fabric-data-platform",slug:"healthcare-fabric-data-platform",client:"Healthcare organization",category:"Data Engineering",title:"A Fabric platform, built from scratch",short:"Healthcare on Fabric",lines:["Healthcare.","One Fabric","foundation."],desc:"A Microsoft Fabric platform built from scratch with all six source applications integrated: five REST APIs and one SQL Server. Bronze, Silver and Gold prepare governed data for Salesforce Data Cloud and Power BI reporting.",stat:"Six sources live",statLabel:"Five APIs · one SQL source",techStack:["Microsoft Fabric","Fabric Pipelines","Fabric Notebooks","Delta Lake","SQL Server","REST APIs","Python","Power BI","Salesforce Data Cloud"],lede:"Six applications needed a shared data foundation for a healthcare organization. This Microsoft Fabric platform was built from scratch around configuration, generated pipelines and notebook processing. All five REST applications and the SQL Server source are integrated through Bronze, Silver and Gold. The prepared data now feeds Salesforce Data Cloud for cross-source identity resolution and Power BI for reporting through Direct Lake.",situation:{current:{label:"Starting point",title:"Six applications. A new foundation.",desc:"The brief called for five API integrations and one SQL Server integration, with consistent data for a CDP and reporting. The platform needed to establish ingestion, contracts, quality checks, run history and deployment from the ground up."},target:{label:"Delivered",title:"Six sources. Salesforce and reporting.",desc:"All six integrations run through the shared Fabric framework, with generated orchestration, recorded checks and isolated rejects. Gold data is ready and in use for Salesforce Data Cloud and Power BI. The reporting model reads Gold Delta tables through Direct Lake; cross-source identity resolution remains in Salesforce."}},architecture:"healthcare-fabric-data-platform",partsTitle:"From configuration to handover",parts:[{title:"Configuration is the framework",desc:"One workspace contains separate schema-enabled lakehouses for Control, Bronze, Silver and Gold. Application, entity and column definitions live as reviewed JSON and CSV in the repository. Deployment applies that configuration, and the generator produces the pipeline definitions. Runtime batches, entity runs, watermarks, quality results and audit records live in the Control lakehouse. All five REST applications and the SQL Server application use this shared framework.",meta:["Generated pipelines","Control lakehouse","JSON + CSV"]},{title:"Two ingestion paths into Bronze",desc:"SQL Server lands through an on-premises gateway and a generated Copy activity per connection. The REST notebook framework integrates all five API applications through source-specific configuration. It supports OAuth refresh-token rotation, incremental reads by modification time and a weekly full-reload path. Inactive and deceased source records are included. Files land first, then merge into Delta using null-safe business keys and a row-loss guard. Notebooks open and close each batch; every entity receives its own run record.",meta:["Fabric Copy activity","REST notebooks","Files to Delta"]},{title:"Silver rebuilds a consistent contract",desc:"A Fabric pipeline invokes the Silver build notebook to rebuild each table from Bronze, with failures isolated by table. Names become snake_case, columns receive explicit types and source-local timestamps become UTC. Sentinel values and Y/N flags are normalized; nested JSON arrays become child tables. Contracts, row rules and relationships are configuration, so transformations and validation can be reviewed together.",meta:["Fabric Notebooks","Full table rebuilds","UTC + typed columns"]},{title:"Every quality check leaves a record",desc:"Each check writes a result to runtime.data_quality. Invalid rows are retained in table-specific reject outputs, separate from accepted Silver data. A build lock and a batch precondition block processing when Bronze has failed or is stale. Rules are added from measured evidence, with their results retained for investigation. Classification also supplies column comments, keeping the data contract visible to the next engineer.",meta:["Recorded checks","Reject tables","Fresh-batch gate"]},{title:"Gold serves Salesforce and reporting",desc:"Gold publishes person records at one row per person per source, retaining native identifiers. Salesforce Data Cloud ingests the selected tables through its Fabric connector and a schema-scoped SQL analytics endpoint connection. Cross-source identity resolution happens in Salesforce Data Cloud. The Power BI semantic model uses Direct Lake on the same Gold lakehouse, reading Delta data from OneLake through a separate consumption path.",meta:["Gold data in use","CDP identity resolution","Power BI Direct Lake"]},{title:"A run is accounted for, end to end",desc:"The parent pipeline processes load groups sequentially, invokes Bronze and starts Silver only after success. Invoke Pipeline activities use the workspace identity. Notification notebooks compose application-grouped summaries from the control tables: one parent Bronze summary, a Silver summary, quality alerts when checks find issues and a failure alert when a run fails. Office 365 Email uses its authenticated connection; message copies are retained with the run evidence.",meta:["Concurrency 1","Workspace identity","Run-based notifications"]},{title:"An engineered handover",desc:"Notebooks, configuration, SQL, deployment scripts and architecture documentation live in the repository, with 77 offline framework tests. Deployment and run tools support notebook logs and Silver-run verification; a status file records the handover. The maintenance notebook is prepared for weekly OPTIMIZE, VACUUM with seven-day retention and runtime-history retention. Large tables use Z-Order.",meta:["77 offline framework tests","Deployment tooling","Maintenance notebook"]}],changes:[{tag:"Foundation",title:"All six sources share the framework.",desc:"Five REST applications and one SQL Server application are integrated through the same configuration-driven Fabric platform."},{tag:"Quality",title:"Accepted data has evidence.",desc:"Silver tables have explicit contracts and recorded checks. Rejected rows remain inspectable, and failed or stale Bronze batches cannot silently feed a new build."},{tag:"Operations",title:"A run tells its own story.",desc:"Batch and entity records connect ingestion, watermarks, quality results and notification copies. Failures produce an alert instead of an ordinary completion summary."},{tag:"Handover",title:"The implementation is reproducible.",desc:"Reviewed configuration generates the pipelines. Deployment scripts, offline tests, run verification and the status handover document accompany the notebooks."},{tag:"Consumption",title:"Salesforce and reporting use the data.",desc:"Salesforce Data Cloud consumes the prepared source-level person tables for CDP identity resolution. Power BI reads the same Gold lakehouse through Direct Lake for reporting."}]},{id:"lakehouse-plant-telemetry",slug:"lakehouse-plant-telemetry",client:"European manufacturing group",category:"Data Engineering",title:"Lakehouse for plant telemetry",short:"Plant telemetry",lines:["Lakehouse for","plant telemetry."],desc:"A Databricks lakehouse for a manufacturing group: line telemetry and ERP data land continuously, are refined by Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines with quality expectations, governed once in Unity Catalog, and read by every plant through Databricks SQL and the same Power BI measures.",stat:"One lakehouse, every plant",statLabel:"Telemetry and ERP, governed once",techStack:["Databricks","Unity Catalog","Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines","Auto Loader","Lakeflow Jobs","Databricks SQL","Power BI","Asset Bundles"],lede:"Plant telemetry arrived as files from line controllers and ERP extracts, each site with its own scripts and its own definition of downtime. We built one lakehouse on Databricks: raw data landing as it arrives, refined by declarative pipelines that enforce quality rules, governed once in Unity Catalog, and read by every plant through the same measures.",situation:{current:{label:"Before",title:"A lakehouse per site, in scripts",desc:"Each plant ran its own extracts and notebooks. Line telemetry sat in files nobody reconciled; ERP data was joined to it by hand in spreadsheets. Downtime meant something slightly different at every site, so group reporting was weeks of reconciliation and numbers nobody quite trusted."},target:{label:"After",title:"One lakehouse, declared",desc:"Telemetry and ERP land continuously into bronze Delta tables, are refined into silver and gold by declarative pipelines with quality expectations, governed once in Unity Catalog, and served through a serverless Databricks SQL warehouse. Every plant reads the same tables and the same measures."}},architecture:"lakehouse-plant-telemetry",parts:[{title:"Continuous landing",desc:"Auto Loader picks up line controller files as they arrive; Lakeflow Connect pulls the ERP tables incrementally. Everything lands in bronze Delta tables with its source, its file and its arrival time. Nothing is transformed on the way in, so any silver rule can be replayed from bronze.",meta:["Auto Loader","Lakeflow Connect","Bronze Delta tables"]},{title:"Declarative pipelines with expectations",desc:"Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines builds the silver and gold layers as streaming tables and materialized views defined in SQL. Expectations validate the data; an explicitly authored quarantine branch retains invalid rows outside the reporting path. Downtime, the OEE inputs and the shift calendars are defined once, in code, and reviewed like code.",meta:["Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines","Expectations","Streaming tables"]},{title:"Governed in Unity Catalog",desc:"One metastore, catalogs per environment, schemas per domain. Row filters keep each plant to its own lines, column masks cover supplier prices, lineage shows where a gold number came from, and the system tables show what each pipeline costs.",meta:["Unity Catalog","Row filters and masks","Lineage"]},{title:"Served to the plants",desc:"A serverless Databricks SQL warehouse serves Power BI over the gold tables, and the plant dashboards read one semantic model. Liquid clustering on the largest telemetry tables keeps line-level queries fast without a maintenance calendar.",meta:["Databricks SQL","Serverless","Liquid clustering"]},{title:"Operations",desc:"Lakeflow Jobs schedule what is not streaming. Databricks Asset Bundles carry pipelines, jobs and permissions from development to production through GitHub Actions, and Lakehouse Monitoring watches freshness and volume per table and alerts on drift.",meta:["Lakeflow Jobs","Asset Bundles","Lakehouse Monitoring"]}],changes:[{tag:"Definitions",title:"Downtime means one thing.",desc:"The rules live in the pipeline code, reviewed and versioned; every plant reads the same gold tables, so group reporting is a query, not a reconciliation."},{tag:"Freshness",title:"Telemetry lands as it happens.",desc:"Streaming tables refresh continuously; a line stop shows in the dashboard within the pipeline’s interval, not the next morning."},{tag:"Quality",title:"Bad rows are quarantined, not loaded.",desc:"Expectations catch malformed readings and duplicate ERP extracts; the run completes and the quarantine is reviewed."},{tag:"Governance",title:"Access is defined once.",desc:"Row filters and column masks govern access to the lakehouse. The Power BI serving path must carry the appropriate user identity or enforce its own report access rules."},{tag:"Change",title:"Every change goes through a bundle.",desc:"Pipelines, jobs and permissions are deployed from Git; nothing is edited in production by hand."}]},{id:"streaming-parcel-events",slug:"streaming-parcel-events",client:"European parcel carrier",category:"Data Engineering",title:"Parcel events, in motion",short:"Streaming events",lines:["Parcel events,","in motion."],desc:"Scanner and vehicle events streamed from Kafka into a Databricks lakehouse: Structured Streaming into bronze, Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines with watermarks and stateful joins into a live parcel status, served to the control room through Databricks SQL and to the tracking API through a Lakebase synced table.",stat:"Status while it moves",statLabel:"Streams, not overnight loads",techStack:["Databricks","Apache Kafka","Structured Streaming","Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines","Delta Lake","Unity Catalog","Databricks SQL","Lakebase","Lakehouse Monitoring"],lede:"A parcel carrier knew where a parcel had been the next morning, when the overnight load ran. We built the flow that knows where it is now: scanner and vehicle events read from Kafka by Structured Streaming into bronze, refined by declarative streaming tables that bound event-time state and handle late scans explicitly, joined statefully into one parcel status, and served both to the control room and to the customer tracking API while the parcel is still moving.",situation:{current:{label:"Before",title:"Yesterday’s position, this morning",desc:"Scanner events and vehicle telemetry went to Kafka and from there to files, loaded overnight into a warehouse. The control room saw yesterday; the tracking API read a table refreshed a few times a day; an exception was found when a customer called."},target:{label:"After",title:"One status, updated as events arrive",desc:"Structured Streaming lands every topic in bronze with checkpointed Delta writes; declarative streaming tables parse, validate and join the scans to the vehicle positions within watermarks; a gold parcel status refreshes continuously; Databricks SQL serves the control room and a Lakebase synced table serves the tracking API’s point lookups."}},architecture:"streaming-parcel-events",parts:[{title:"Checkpointed Kafka into bronze",desc:"Structured Streaming reads each topic into its own bronze Delta table with the raw payload, key, partition, offset and ingest time. Checkpoints and Delta’s transactional commits support exactly-once recovery; event-key deduplication handles repeated business events separately. The raw payload retains fields that have not yet been promoted into the parsed schema.",meta:["Structured Streaming","Bronze per topic","Checkpointed Delta writes"]},{title:"Streaming tables with watermarks",desc:"Silver is Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines streaming tables: payloads parsed and validated with expectations, duplicates dropped on the event key, and a watermark that bounds the event-time state retained for processing. Raw events remain in bronze; explicit late-event handling flags scans outside the state horizon for review.",meta:["Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines","Expectations","Watermarks"]},{title:"Stateful joins into one status",desc:"Scans are joined to vehicle positions in a stream-stream join within a time window, and a stateful aggregation keeps the current status per parcel: last scan, last known position, next expected event and its deadline. The gold table is the answer to “where is it”, kept by the pipeline rather than recomputed by every reader.",meta:["Stream-stream join","Stateful aggregation","Gold parcel status"]},{title:"Serving at two speeds",desc:"The control room reads the gold table through a serverless Databricks SQL warehouse, with SQL alerts on parcels past their expected event. The tracking API reads a Lakebase table synced from the same gold table, so a customer’s lookup is a Postgres point read and never touches the warehouse.",meta:["Databricks SQL","SQL alerts","Lakebase synced table"]},{title:"Operations",desc:"Lakehouse Monitoring watches freshness and volume per table and alerts when a topic goes quiet or a watermark starts closing too much state. Pipelines and their compute are declared in Asset Bundles and promoted through Git; a schema change is a reviewed pull request, not a hotfix at three in the morning.",meta:["Lakehouse Monitoring","Asset Bundles","Reviewed schema changes"]}],changes:[{tag:"Latency",title:"The status moves with the parcel.",desc:"Events reach the gold table within the pipeline’s interval, so the control room and the tracking API describe now rather than yesterday."},{tag:"Correctness",title:"Recovery preserves committed writes.",desc:"Checkpointed recovery and transactional Delta writes avoid duplicating committed output; event-key deduplication separately handles repeated scans."},{tag:"Late data",title:"Late scans are kept, not lost.",desc:"Watermarks bound event-time state. Raw scans remain in bronze, and a separate late-event path flags records that need review."},{tag:"Exceptions",title:"A stalled parcel raises itself.",desc:"SQL alerts fire on parcels past their expected event; the control room acts before the customer calls."},{tag:"Serving",title:"Lookups never touch the warehouse.",desc:"The tracking API reads the Lakebase synced table; the analysts’ queries and the customers’ lookups no longer compete."}]},{id:"synapse-to-databricks-migration",slug:"synapse-to-databricks-migration",client:"Regional insurer",category:"Migration",title:"Synapse estate moved to Databricks",short:"Synapse to Databricks",lines:["Synapse to","Databricks."],desc:"A Synapse dedicated pool, its ADF pipelines and notebooks moved to Databricks with Lakebridge: the T-SQL translated and reviewed, every table reconciled before its old copy was switched off, orchestration rebuilt as Lakeflow Jobs, Power BI repointed without the reports changing.",stat:"The estate, reconciled",statLabel:"Moved object by object",techStack:["Databricks","Lakebridge","Unity Catalog","Lakeflow Jobs","Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines","Databricks SQL","Delta Sharing","Power BI"],lede:"An insurer ran claims and policy reporting on a Synapse dedicated pool with ADF pipelines around it and a retirement date approaching. We moved the estate to Databricks with Lakebridge: the analyzer inventoried it, the converter translated the T-SQL for engineers to review, and each table passed reconciliation checks before its pool copy was switched off.",situation:{current:{label:"Before",title:"A pool with a retirement date",desc:"Claims and policy marts in a Synapse dedicated pool, loaded by ADF pipelines and a few notebooks; stored procedures nobody wanted to touch; Power BI refreshing from all of it every night. The pool was billed at rest and slow at month end."},target:{label:"After",title:"Databricks, proven table by table",desc:"Delta tables in Unity Catalog, procedures translated to Databricks SQL and reviewed, ADF orchestration rebuilt as Lakeflow Jobs, the semantic models repointed to a serverless SQL warehouse. Each object was reconciled against the pool across a full reporting cycle before the pool lost it."}},architecture:"synapse-to-databricks-migration",parts:[{title:"Assessment with Lakebridge",desc:"Assessment inventoried the pool and pipelines with the Lakebridge analyzer: tables, procedures, views and their dependencies. The estate was ranked by use, and the part of it that nothing read was retired without being migrated at all.",meta:["Lakebridge analyzer","Inventory","Dead objects retired"]},{title:"Translation, reviewed",desc:"The converter translated T-SQL procedures and views to Databricks SQL; engineers reviewed every diff, kept the business logic and rewrote only the dialect. Nothing was translated blind, and the reviewed SQL went into Git with its history.",meta:["Lakebridge converter","T-SQL to Databricks SQL","Reviewed diffs"]},{title:"Orchestration",desc:"ADF pipelines became Lakeflow Jobs with the same schedules and dependencies; the loads that were really transformations became Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines with expectations, so a month-end run no longer failed on one bad extract.",meta:["Lakeflow Jobs","Declarative Pipelines","Same schedules"]},{title:"Reconciliation",desc:"The reconciler compared row counts, aggregates and samples between the pool and Databricks for every table on every run, until the numbers matched across a full reporting cycle. Only then was a table cut over and its pool copy frozen.",meta:["Lakebridge reconciler","Every table","A full cycle"]},{title:"Serving and sharing",desc:"The Power BI models were repointed to a serverless Databricks SQL warehouse, measures and reports untouched. Reinsurers who received extract files now read governed tables through Delta Sharing.",meta:["Databricks SQL","Delta Sharing","Reports unchanged"]}],changes:[{tag:"Cutover",title:"Nothing was switched off on faith.",desc:"Row counts, aggregates and sample comparisons agreed across a full reporting cycle before each table’s pool copy was frozen."},{tag:"Logic",title:"The procedures kept their meaning.",desc:"Translation changed the dialect; reviewers kept the business rules, and the SQL now lives in Git."},{tag:"Month end",title:"Runs finish on bad data too.",desc:"Expectations validate each extract; explicit quarantine logic retains malformed records while valid data continues through the pipeline."},{tag:"Cost",title:"Compute runs when there is work.",desc:"Serverless SQL and job clusters replace a pool billed at rest; the system tables show what each job costs."},{tag:"Partners",title:"Shares instead of files.",desc:"Reinsurers read governed tables through Delta Sharing; the extract jobs and their failures are gone."}]},{id:"governed-knowledge-agent",slug:"governed-knowledge-agent",client:"Professional services firm",category:"AI Engineering",title:"A governed knowledge agent",short:"Knowledge agent",lines:["A governed","knowledge agent."],desc:"A retrieval agent on Databricks for a professional services firm: engagement documents managed in Unity Catalog and searched through a permission-aware retriever over Mosaic AI Vector Search, served through Model Serving behind the AI Gateway, evaluated with MLflow before every release, answering with its sources.",stat:"Answers with sources",statLabel:"Permission-aware retrieval",techStack:["Databricks","Mosaic AI Vector Search","Agent Framework","Model Serving","AI Gateway","MLflow","Unity Catalog","Databricks Apps"],lede:"Consultants searched years of engagement documents by memory and by asking around. We built an agent on Databricks that answers with the sources: documents ingested as they are saved, chunked and embedded into Vector Search, retrieved with the reader’s document permissions checked before passages reach the model, and served behind the AI Gateway with every request logged and every release evaluated.",situation:{current:{label:"Before",title:"Knowledge in people’s heads",desc:"Proposals, deliverables and lessons learned sat in document libraries by client and year. Finding the last comparable engagement meant asking a partner. Nothing could be opened to a model without opening everything to everyone."},target:{label:"After",title:"An agent that cites and respects permissions",desc:"Documents indexed in Vector Search inside Unity Catalog, with a permission-aware retriever controlling which passages reach the model; answers from a foundation model through Model Serving with the passages cited; every request through the AI Gateway with logging, rate limits and guardrails; every release gated by evaluation."}},architecture:"governed-knowledge-agent",parts:[{title:"Ingestion",desc:"Auto Loader lands new documents as they are saved. A Lakeflow pipeline parses, chunks and embeds them into Delta tables, and a Vector Search index with Delta Sync follows those tables, so the index is never rebuilt by hand.",meta:["Auto Loader","Chunking and embedding","Delta Sync index"]},{title:"Retrieval under permissions",desc:"The retriever checks the reader’s document permissions before passages reach the model. The shared index requires explicit permission filtering; row filters on the underlying chunk tables alone do not secure vector query results.",meta:["Vector Search","Permission-aware retrieval","One index"]},{title:"The agent",desc:"Built with the Mosaic AI Agent Framework: retrieval, a model from the Foundation Model APIs, and tools that read engagement metadata. Deployed to Model Serving with MLflow tracing on every request, so a bad answer can be read back step by step.",meta:["Agent Framework","Foundation Model APIs","Model Serving"]},{title:"Evaluation as a release gate",desc:"MLflow evaluation runs a curated question set with LLM judges and expert-reviewed answers before each release; a version that scores below the current one does not ship. Production traces feed the next question set.",meta:["MLflow evaluation","LLM judges","Release gate"]},{title:"Gateway and interface",desc:"The AI Gateway fronts the endpoint with rate limits, payload logging and guardrails. Consultants use the agent through a Databricks App that shows the answer and the cited passages side by side.",meta:["AI Gateway","Guardrails","Databricks App"]}],changes:[{tag:"Access",title:"One index, everyone’s own permissions.",desc:"Permission-aware retrieval limits the passages available to the agent to documents the consultant is allowed to read."},{tag:"Trust",title:"Every answer shows its sources.",desc:"Passages are cited and one click away; an answer without a source is not shown."},{tag:"Quality",title:"Releases are gated by evaluation.",desc:"A version that scores lower than the current one on the question set does not ship."},{tag:"Operations",title:"Every request is logged.",desc:"Gateway logs and traces show what was asked, what was retrieved and what it cost, per team."},{tag:"Freshness",title:"New documents are searchable the same day.",desc:"The index follows the Delta tables; nothing is re-indexed by hand."}]},{id:"pricing-desk-databricks-app",slug:"pricing-desk-databricks-app",client:"Building materials distributor",category:"Analytics",title:"A pricing desk on Databricks Apps",short:"Pricing desk",lines:["A pricing desk","on Databricks Apps."],desc:"An internal pricing application built on Databricks Apps for a distributor: a Dash app that reads the gold tables through a serverless SQL warehouse as the signed-in analyst, shows a demand model’s proposals from Model Serving, and writes decisions to Lakebase with a full audit trail, deployed with Asset Bundles.",stat:"Decisions inside the platform",statLabel:"No spreadsheet round trip",techStack:["Databricks Apps","Dash","Databricks SQL","Unity Catalog","Lakebase","Model Serving","MLflow","Asset Bundles"],lede:"Regional pricing lived in spreadsheets emailed around every week and pasted back into the ERP by hand. We built the desk where the decisions are made, as a Databricks App: the analyst sees margin, stock and the demand model’s proposal for their own region, decides, and the decision is written to Lakebase with who, when and why, then synced back into the lakehouse where the reports read it.",situation:{current:{label:"Before",title:"Prices by spreadsheet, weekly",desc:"Analysts exported margin and stock reports, set prices in a spreadsheet, emailed it around for sign-off and pasted the result into the ERP. Nobody could say later who changed what, or why; the demand forecast lived in a notebook nobody opened."},target:{label:"After",title:"One desk, inside the platform",desc:"A Databricks App where the analyst works on their own region’s rows, sees the demand model’s proposal beside the current price, decides, and the decision is written to Lakebase with an audit trail and synced into Delta for the reports. Authorization runs on behalf of the user, so the catalog’s row filters apply inside the app."}},architecture:"pricing-desk-databricks-app",figure:{id:"pricing-desk",label:"The app",eyebrow:"The app",title:"The desk, as the analyst sees it",note:"A representative view of the app: the analyst’s own region, the current price beside the demand model’s proposal, and every decision written to Lakebase with who, when and why, then synced to Delta. Left alone it works through the list; select a row to decide it yourself."},parts:[{title:"The app",desc:"A Dash application hosted on Databricks Apps, next to the data instead of in another cloud. Its resources are declared with it: a serverless SQL warehouse to read, a Lakebase database to write, a Model Serving endpoint for the proposals. Sign-in is the workspace’s own; there is no second identity to manage.",meta:["Databricks Apps","Dash","Declared resources"]},{title:"Reads as the analyst",desc:"Queries run on behalf of the signed-in user, so the row filters and column masks defined once in Unity Catalog apply inside the app: a regional analyst sees their region’s rows and nobody else’s, and the app has no privileged path around the catalog.",meta:["On-behalf-of-user authorization","Row filters","Databricks SQL"]},{title:"The proposal",desc:"A demand model trained on the lakehouse and tracked in MLflow serves proposals from a Model Serving endpoint. The app shows the proposal beside the current price and the margin it implies; the analyst decides, the model does not. Every proposal and every decision is logged for the next training run.",meta:["Model Serving","MLflow","Human decision"]},{title:"Decisions written to Lakebase",desc:"A decision is a row in Lakebase, the platform’s Postgres: price, effective date, who, when, why. A separate change-ingestion path carries these decisions into Delta in Unity Catalog, so reports and the ERP integration read the same decision the analyst made, with lineage back to the app.",meta:["Lakebase","Audit trail","Change ingestion to Delta"]},{title:"Delivery and operations",desc:"The app, its resources and its permissions are one Databricks Asset Bundle, promoted from development to production through Git. App logs and the platform’s system tables show who used it, what it queried and what it cost; a release is a pull request.",meta:["Asset Bundles","Git","System tables"]}],changes:[{tag:"Workflow",title:"The decision is made where the data is.",desc:"No export, no email round, no paste into the ERP; the desk reads the gold tables and writes the decision."},{tag:"Governance",title:"The app cannot see more than the analyst.",desc:"On-behalf-of-user authorization keeps the catalog’s row filters in force inside the app."},{tag:"Audit",title:"Every price has a who, a when and a why.",desc:"Decisions live in Lakebase and sync to Delta; a question about last quarter’s price is a query."},{tag:"Model",title:"The forecast is used, not admired.",desc:"Proposals appear beside the decision; what the analyst chose feeds the next training run."},{tag:"Delivery",title:"The app ships like the pipelines.",desc:"One bundle, one review, one promotion path; no separate hosting to secure."}]},{id:"metadata-driven-ingestion-framework",slug:"metadata-driven-ingestion-framework",client:"National grocery chain",category:"Data Engineering",title:"Metadata-driven ingestion and Power BI",short:"Ingestion framework",lines:["Metadata-driven","ingestion."],desc:"A metadata-driven ingestion framework on Azure Data Factory and Synapse: new sources are onboarded by configuration rather than by building another pipeline, and store managers read current stock data in Power BI.",stat:"One framework, every source",statLabel:"Ingestion by configuration",techStack:["Azure Data Factory","Synapse Analytics","Power BI","Python","Azure SQL"],lede:"A grocery estate ingested supplier and point-of-sale data through hand-written SSIS packages, one per feed, loaded overnight. We replaced the estate with one master pipeline that reads its instructions from a control table, curated the result in Synapse, and put a governed Power BI dataset in front of store managers.",situation:{current:{label:"Before",title:"One package per feed",desc:"A large estate of hand-written SSIS packages, each supplier and each point-of-sale feed with its own. A new supplier meant a new package, a deployment and one more thing to maintain. Loads ran overnight, so the stock reports store managers relied on described yesterday."},target:{label:"After",title:"One pipeline, driven by a table",desc:"One reusable master pipeline in Azure Data Factory, told what to load by a control table. A new source is a row in that table, reviewed like any other change. Data lands append-only, is curated in Synapse, and is read by store managers through one governed Power BI dataset."}},architecture:"metadata-driven-ingestion-framework",parts:[{title:"The control table",desc:"One row per source in Azure SQL: system, connection, schedule, schema, landing path, quality rules. The table is the specification of the estate, versioned and reviewed; nothing about a source lives only inside a pipeline.",meta:["Azure SQL","One row per source","Reviewed like code"]},{title:"The master pipeline",desc:"A single Azure Data Factory pipeline reads the control table and runs each source through the same steps: extract, land, validate, log. Parameterised activities and loops replace copies of the same logic; schema drift is handled in configuration, not in a hotfix.",meta:["Azure Data Factory","Parameterised","Schema drift in config"]},{title:"Bronze, silver, gold",desc:"Bronze retains every feed append-only in its original form. Synapse validates and conforms it into silver cleansed tables, then builds the gold star schema for the questions store teams actually ask: stock, sell-through, expiry. Each layer has a distinct responsibility before Power BI reads the reporting marts.",meta:["Bronze raw landing","Silver cleansed tables","Gold star schema"]},{title:"The store manager dataset",desc:"One governed Power BI dataset over the star schema, with the measures defined once. Store managers see current stock and near-expiry lines in the report they open every morning; head office sees the same numbers.",meta:["Power BI","One dataset","Same numbers everywhere"]},{title:"Operations",desc:"Every run is logged against the source that produced it, with alerts on failed validations. Onboarding, schedule changes and retirements are all changes to the control table, so the operations story is one table and one pipeline.",meta:["Run logs per source","Validation alerts","One thing to operate"]}],changes:[{tag:"Onboarding",title:"A new source is configuration.",desc:"A row in the control table, reviewed and released, and the master pipeline loads it on its schedule. No new package, no new deployment."},{tag:"Operations",title:"One pipeline to run and version.",desc:"The estate of packages became one pipeline and one table. Monitoring, alerting and change control apply once."},{tag:"Freshness",title:"Schedules set per source.",desc:"Loads are no longer one overnight batch; each source runs on the cadence its data needs, so store managers read current stock rather than yesterday’s."},{tag:"Self-service",title:"One governed dataset.",desc:"Store teams and head office read the same Power BI dataset with the same measures; the spreadsheet copies that used to circulate went away."},{tag:"Retirement",title:"The SSIS estate was retired source by source.",desc:"Each feed was moved onto the framework, reconciled against its package, and the package switched off. Nothing was retired on faith."}]}]],82356)}]);